**THE PORT HAD NO AVX-512 TIER AT ALL, AND THE WIENER CONVOLVE HAD NO VECTOR
ARM ON EITHER ISA.** `stall_attrib_2026-09-05` §4 found that valgrind masks
AVX-512, so every callgrind number in this campaign priced C's AVX2 arm while
the real hardware ran `svt_av1_wiener_convolve_add_src_avx512` and five
siblings — and that the port answered with **zero** `_v4` / `X64V4Token` /
`avx512` occurrences anywhere in `crates/svtav1-dsp/src`. This commit turns the
tier on and spends it on the ranking's row 5, the Wiener trial filter, which was
**53.65x C by cycles** because `restoration.rs`'s horizontal and vertical rows
were plain scalar `for` loops. The kernel goes **57,872,128 -> 13,906,583 Ir
(-75.97 %, 52.78x C -> 12.68x)** and the change buys **1.029x / 1.051x / 1.034x
wall clock** on photo_cid, screen_terminal and gradient at p6, every span
disjoint from its same-binary control, every cell byte-identical.

date:   2026-09-05 (runs 18:00-19:00 UTC)
hosts:  MEASUREMENT r7900x (x86_64-linux, AMD Ryzen 9 7900X, **Zen 4 —
        AVX-512 double-pumped over 256-bit units, see §4b**, 24 threads,
        Linux 7.0.0-30; `avx512f dq cd bw vl vbmi vbmi2 vnni bitalg vpopcntdq
        gfni vaes vpclmulqdq bf16` all present). Load 0.38-0.57 at the timing
        launches and 0.80-1.10 at the end; the `zenav1-svt--cdef1` lane is on
        this box, so every control and its A/B were taken back-to-back in ONE
        session under the SAME conditions. GATES both ISAs (aarch64 = this Mac,
        M4 Pro).
base:   `origin/main 84621b20` — perf(cdef) find_dir, immediately prior.
cand:   this commit. Two files: `crates/svtav1-dsp/src/restoration.rs` and
        `crates/svtav1-dsp/Cargo.toml`.
C:      reference/svt-av1 @ 3115c0c1, the PREBUILT oracle
        (`rust/tools/perf_c_encode/perf_c_encode`, 2026-09-03), NOT rebuilt.
data:   benchmarks/wiener_avx512_tier_2026-09-05.tsv.

=================== 1. HOW THE TIER IS ENABLED, AND HOW IT IS GATED ===================

Three facts, each verified rather than inferred:

  * **archmage already tries `_v4` — the port never gave it the feature.**
    `incant!`'s default ladder includes `_v4` "if the `avx512` feature is
    enabled", and `resolve_tiers(.., default_feature_gates = true)`
    (`archmage-macros/src/tiers.rs:420`) auto-attaches
    `#[cfg(feature = "avx512")]` to a bare `v4` in BOTH `incant!` and
    `#[magetypes]`. The feature it tests is the CALLING CRATE's, not archmage's.
    `zenav1-svt-dsp` had no such feature, so `v4` could never have appeared even
    if a tier list had asked for it.
  * **The feature is DEFAULT-ON, deliberately.** `crates/svtav1-dsp/Cargo.toml`
    gains `default = ["avx512"]`, `avx512 = ["archmage/avx512",
    "magetypes/avx512"]`. Turning it on does not force AVX-512 on anyone: it
    makes `X64V4Token` exist so the `_v4` arm is COMPILED, and whether that arm
    RUNS is decided at run time by `X64V4Token::summon()` reading CPUID. Both
    dependents (`svtav1-encoder`, `svtav1`) take the dsp crate with default
    features, so the tier reaches the gates, the benches and the CLI. Making it
    opt-in would have shipped a tier nobody ever enables — this repo's
    most-repeated defect wearing a Cargo feature.
  * **Runtime detection is PROVEN, from both sides.** On r7900x the new unit
    test prints the tiers the dispatch actually resolves to across archmage's
    token permutations: `["X64V4Token", "X64V3Token", "ScalarToken"]`. On the
    M4 Pro the same test prints `["NeonToken", "ScalarToken"]` — the `_v4` arm
    is `#[cfg(target_arch = "x86_64")]`'d out of existence there. And under
    valgrind, which masks AVX-512 in CPUID, the callgrind profile names
    `wiener_convolve_simd_v3` and never `_v4`: the summon returns None and the
    ladder steps down one tier with no fault. That last point is not a detail —
    it is the answer to "does a binary that emits AVX-512 crash where AVX-512 is
    absent", and the answer is measured NO on a live CPUID-masking host.

=================== 2. WHY THIS KERNEL, AND NOT ANOTHER ===================

The brief named four candidates from the cycle ranking. Wiener convolve was
taken first for three reasons that survived checking:

  * It is the **highest remaining ratio** — 53.65x C's cycles, +3.16 % of the
    p6 frame — and CDEF (+5.74 %) is a live sibling lane.
  * **C has an AVX-512 kernel for exactly it** (`svt_av1_wiener_convolve_add_src_avx512`),
    so the oracle for a 512-bit design is written down.
  * `compute_stats` next door is now 1.349x C (`compute_stats_x86_recheck_2026-09-05`),
    so the LR stage's remaining cost really is concentrated here.

**A CORRECTION TO THE BRIEF.** It framed this as "add a `_v4` arm". The kernel
had **no vector arm at all, on either ISA** — the `incant!` did not exist and
`wiener_h_row` / `wiener_v_row` were scalar `for x in 0..w` loops with an inner
8-tap loop. So this commit is not an AVX-512 tier bolted onto an AVX2 kernel; it
is the kernel's first vectorisation, delivered on five tiers at once, of which
`_v4` is one. That also means aarch64 is NOT unaffected: it gains a NEON arm
from the same body. The gates below cover it.

`spatial_full_distortion` was left alone, as the brief permitted: the port is
already cheaper than C there, and adding a tier for symmetry rather than for a
measurement is exactly what the campaign's rules forbid.

=================== 3. WHAT LANDED ===================

`crates/svtav1-dsp/src/restoration.rs`:

  * `wiener_convolve_simd` — ONE `#[magetypes(define(i32x16), v4(cfg(avx512)),
    v3, neon, wasm128, scalar)]` body. Five tiers from one algorithm: no
    per-ISA transliteration, no second design to keep in sync.
  * `wiener_simd_applicable` — the precondition, checked per call.
  * `wiener_simd_tier_name` / `wiener_simd_tier` — `#[cfg(test)]` witnesses that
    report which arm the ladder selects.
  * The dispatch, three lines at the top of `wiener_convolve_add_src`. The
    scalar streamed path below it is UNCHANGED and remains the fallback.

**Why `#[magetypes]` here when `intra_pred`, `residual` and `me_sad` each carry
a "why not `#[magetypes]`" note.** Those notes are correct and stay: they need
an integer WIDENING conversion, and PUBLISHED magetypes 0.9.28 — the one in
`Cargo.lock` — has none. That was verified with `cargo read magetypes`, NOT from
the local `~/work/archmage` checkout, which carries unpublished `widen_low` /
`narrow_saturating_*` work that crates.io does not have. **The first attempt at
this kernel used those unpublished methods, compiled against the local checkout
in my head, and failed to build against the registry crate — the trap the
"docs almost always lie" rule exists for, in its most literal form.**

The landed design sidesteps the gap instead of needing the patch: it never
converts between lane widths. Every value is `i32x16` from the moment the source
byte is read, and the `u8 -> i32` widening is a plain
`for t { s32[t] = i32::from(src[..]) }` loop that LLVM lowers to `vpmovzxbd`
inside each tier's `#[target_feature]` region. The whole magetypes surface used
is `i32x16::{splat, from_slice, store, min, max, shr_arithmetic_const}` plus
`Add` and `Mul<i32>` — all published, and all backed by `type Repr = __m512i`
for `X64V4Token`, which is why the `_v4` arm really is 512 bits wide (108 `zmm`
instructions against the `_v3` arm's 89 `ymm`; `objdump` census in the .tsv).

**The oracle**, `Source/Lib/ASM_AVX512/wiener_convolve_avx512.c`:

  * `svt_av1_wiener_convolve_add_src_avx512:270` is the entry; `:296` folds the
    `add_src` term into the vertical coefficient (`coeffs_y + offset_0`, a
    `1 << FILTER_BITS` planted at lane 3). **Reproduced, on both passes** — the
    centre column becomes `r3 * (f[3] + 128)` instead of a separate `r3 << 7`,
    one multiply cheaper per pass and an exact regrouping of the scalar row.
  * `wiener_convolve_v_tap7_kernel_avx512:191` adds `s[0]+s[6]`, `s[1]+s[5]`,
    `s[2]+s[4]` before multiplying, four products instead of eight.
    **Reproduced**, behind the symmetry precondition.
  * `wiener_clip_avx512:27` shifts the tap sum BEFORE adding the centre sample
    so C's horizontal pass fits in i16 lanes, and `calc_zero_coef:274`
    specialises to 3/5/7 taps when the outer taps are zero. **NOT reproduced** —
    see §6, they are what is left.

**The precondition** (`wiener_simd_applicable`) certifies two things, and
neither is about rounding, because the vector arm computes the same i32
expression the scalar rows do, term for term:

  1. `w <= WIENER_MAX_PROC_W`. `wiener_filter_stripe` (restoration.c:399) asks
     for `procunit_width.min((stripe_width - j + 15) & !15)` — 16/32/48/64 luma,
     16/32 chroma. A new test asserts every one of those reaches the arm.
  2. The vertical filter is symmetric with `f[7] = 0`, which is what the
     symmetric fold needs. `finalize_sym_filter` (restoration_pick.c:1003) and
     `WienerInfo::default` both guarantee it, but the function is public and
     takes an arbitrary `&[i16; 8]`, so it is CHECKED.

  The `S|f| <= 4096` bounds are not a rounding constraint either. Both arms
  accumulate in i32; magetypes' lane arithmetic WRAPS where the scalar row's
  `i32` add TRAPS in the debug profile `cargo nextest` builds. Real Wiener taps
  give `S|f| <= 286`, so the bound is three orders of magnitude of slack and
  never routes an encoder call to the scalar path.

=================== 4. THE NUMBERS ===================

**WALL CLOCK is the load-bearing evidence here, not Ir** — callgrind cannot
execute AVX-512 and prices the `_v3` arm (§5). `tools/perf_ab.sh`, 21
interleaved randomised paired rounds, neither side niced, every cell ident=Y,
each A/B paired with a SAME-BINARY control (base vs a byte copy of base) in the
same session:

  cell                     CONTROL (p25-p75)          A/B (p25-p75)           net
  photo_cid       512 p6   0.9997 (0.9965-1.0032)  0.9717 (0.9672-0.9738)  **1.029x, DISJOINT**
  screen_terminal 512 p6   0.9985 (0.9877-1.0025)  0.9498 (0.9435-0.9554)  **1.051x, DISJOINT**
  gradient        512 p6   0.9979 (0.9889-1.0078)  0.9655 (0.9562-0.9723)  **1.034x, DISJOINT**
  gradient        512 p2   0.9986 (0.9963-1.0023)  0.9858 (0.9828-0.9880)  **1.013x, DISJOINT**
  photo_cid       512 p2   0.9964 (0.9938-0.9982)  0.9921 (0.9899-0.9943)  ~1.004x, spans TOUCH

The photo_cid p2 row is reported as MARGINAL, not as a win: its two spans share
the interval 0.9938-0.9943. That is the expected shape — at p2 the LR search is
a far smaller share of a 2.3-second frame than of a 73-millisecond one.

**HARDWARE COUNTERS**, `perf stat -e cycles,instructions`, paired warmup-delta
