=================== 4b. THE MICROARCHITECTURE THE NUMBERS ARE FROM ===================

**EVERY figure in §4 is Zen 4 (Ryzen 9 7900X). AVX-512 ON ZEN 4 IS
DOUBLE-PUMPED OVER 256-BIT EXECUTION UNITS — a 512-bit op issues as two
256-bit halves — so a `_v4` arm on this silicon buys instruction-count and
front-end width, not execution width.** That is exactly the shape of what was
measured here: instructions -3.27 % / -5.65 % with IPC essentially unmoved.

**Zen 5 (Ryzen 9 9950X3D) has a FULL-WIDTH 512-bit datapath, and this tier was
NOT MEASURED THERE.** A Zen 5 box (`dev`) exists on this network but was
unavailable during this chunk; `lilith` is another 7950X, i.e. Zen 4 again, so
it would add a quieter room and no new microarchitecture. **Do not read the
numbers above as the tier's ceiling, and do not extrapolate them onto Zen 5 in
either direction** — a 512-bit arm that gains little on a double-pumped part can
gain materially on a full-width one, and this record cannot say which happens.
The honest statement is: **measured on Zen 4, not measured on Zen 5.**

The same caveat applies to any future `_v4` / `_v4x` arm in this port. When one
lands, price it on both generations before claiming a number for "AVX-512".

=================== 5. THE METHODOLOGICAL TRAP, MEASURED ===================

**VALGRIND CANNOT EXECUTE AVX-512, AND IT DOES NOT TRY: IT MASKS THE CPUID
BITS.** That has two consequences, and both were checked on this host rather
than assumed:

  * **Callgrind cannot see the new arm.** Under valgrind `X64V4Token::summon()`
    returns None and `incant!` steps down to `_v3`. The proof is in the profile
    itself: `grep -o 'wiener_convolve_simd_v[34]' cg.out` yields two hits, both
    `_v3`, zero `_v4`. **Every Ir number in §4 therefore prices the AVX2 arm.**
    Any future Ir comparison on an x86 kernel with a `_v4` tier carries the same
    blind spot — use `perf stat` and wall clock, and say which instrument
    produced which number.
  * **The masking is also what makes the tier SAFE under valgrind.** A binary
    that emits AVX-512 and runs it where AVX-512 is absent is a SIGILL, not a
    slowdown. This is the direct test of that: a CPUID-masking host ran the
    candidate under callgrind to completion, byte-identically, on the `_v3` arm.
    The whole campaign's callgrind tooling keeps working unchanged.

=================== 6. WHAT IS LEFT, AND WHERE THE PRIMITIVES ARE MISSING ===================

12.68x C on the AVX2 arm is a big improvement on 52.78x and is NOT the end. Two
of C's rearrangements were not reproduced, and both are blocked on the same
magetypes gap:

  * **The horizontal pass runs 16 lanes where C runs 32.** C keeps it in i16
    (`maddubs` on byte-shuffled data); staying in i32 is what let one body serve
    five tiers without a widening conversion. Closing it needs `madd_adjacent`
    (`_mm512_madd_epi16` / `_mm256_madd_epi16` / `vmlal_s16`) AND a `u8 -> i16`
    widen.
  * **The vertical pass multiplies where C `madd`s.** Same primitive.

Both belong on **archmage issue #89**, which already tracks `madd_adjacent` and
`abs_diff` — no new issue. Two more entries for that list, found here:

  * `u16x32` has no `bitcast_i16x32` (the `u16x16` and `u16x8` forms exist), and
    `U8x32Widen` is not implemented for `X64V4Token`. Between them there is no
    published path from a `u8` load to `i16x32` at 512 bits, which is what
    pushed this kernel to i32 lanes.
  * These are gaps in the LOCAL checkout's newer widen/narrow API too; in
    published 0.9.28 the whole `widen_narrow` backend module is absent.

Also still open, unchanged by this commit: C's `calc_zero_coef` 3/5/7-tap
specialisation, a call-shape optimisation worth revisiting once the lane width
is settled.

Filed as a comment on archmage #89 (no new issue), with the measurement above
as a second data point for `madd_adjacent` alongside `compute_stats`':
https://github.com/imazen/archmage/issues/89#issuecomment-5554056334

=================== 7. EXACTNESS ===================

New unit tests in `restoration.rs`:

  * `wiener_simd_all_tiers_match_materialised` — sweeps EVERY archmage token
    permutation and asserts each equals `wiener_convolve_add_src_materialised`,
    the literal transcription of `svt_av1_wiener_convolve_add_src_c`
    (convolve.c:106), which shares no code with the arm under test. The tap
    domain is covered at its CORNERS, not sampled: all 2^3 combinations of
    `t0/t1/t2` at the AV1 min/max (restoration.h:141-147), plus
    `WienerInfo::default` and the identity `[0,0,0,128,0,0,0,0]` — the one
    filter that would hide an off-by-one in the centre tap. h and v filters are
    deliberately paired unequal so an h/v swap cannot pass. 69 content rounds x
    10 filters x 7 shapes: flat black, flat white, flat grey, xorshift noise, a
    column ramp, and **64 IMPULSE rounds that walk a single 255 across the
    entire 8x8 window**, which is what pins the lane-to-tap mapping (a
    transposed or rotated shuffle survives random data far more often than it
    survives a moving impulse).
    It carries three ANTI-VACUITY assertions: every filter in the list is
    asserted to PASS `wiener_simd_applicable` before the sweep (otherwise the
    test would be scalar-vs-scalar), `permutations_run >= 2` with zero archmage
    warnings, and `>= 2` DISTINCT tiers actually resolved. On x86 with AVX-512 it
    additionally asserts the resolved set CONTAINS `X64V4Token` — so if the
    `avx512` feature is ever dropped from the dependency chain, the test fails
    on the machines that have the hardware instead of silently losing the tier.
  * `wiener_simd_arm_covers_every_encoder_call_shape` — the other direction:
    every proc-unit width the encoder asks for reaches the arm, and the three
    things it must REFUSE (over-wide, asymmetric vertical filter, non-zero tap
    7) are refused.

The pre-existing `wiener_streaming_matches_materialised` now exercises the
vector arm automatically, since `wiener_convolve_add_src` dispatches to it.

**MUTATION-PROVEN, both directions.** Green is not evidence until red has been
seen. Changing ONE character in the vector body — `vc3 = vfilter[3] + (1 <<
FILTER_BITS)` to `+ (1 << FILTER_BITS) + 1`, i.e. perturbing the vertical centre
tap by one — makes `wiener_simd_all_tiers_match_materialised` FAIL immediately,
on the identity filter's very first shape (`16x8`, `hf = vf =
[0,0,0,128,0,0,0,0]`, every output byte wrong). The mutation was reverted and
the test is green again. So the arm is genuinely load-bearing and the assertion
genuinely compares it — this is not a scalar-vs-scalar tautology dressed as a
tier sweep.

**And the arm is what the ENCODER calls, not only the test.** The callgrind
profile is the proof: at `photo_cid` p6 the old `wiener_convolve_add_src` symbol
drops from 57,872,128 self Ir to 52,935 — the dispatch shell and nothing else —
while `__arcane_wiener_convolve_simd_v3` appears with 13,853,648. The encoder's
loop-restoration calls go through the vector arm.

GATES — see §8.

