`video_key_matrix`'s 60th cell and `inter_byte_gate`'s one known-open cell are
pre-existing and unmoved by this commit (the same counts the cdef find_dir
record carries).

**RE-VERIFIED AFTER *EACH* REBASE, because the base moved under this chunk
TWICE.** The
measurements above were taken against `84621b20`; while the gates ran,
`main@origin` advanced to `7ec5b557` (the aarch64 4-wide chroma CDEF arm, plus
a docs commit). The two changes touch disjoint kernels — `cdef.rs` and
`restoration.rs` — but CDEF feeds loop restoration in the pipeline, so
"individually byte-inert" is an argument, not a measurement. On the REBASED
tree, both ISAs: nextest 2546/2546 (arm) / 2556/2556 (x86), the tier witness
still resolving `["X64V4Token","X64V3Token","ScalarToken"]` on x86 and
`["NeonToken","ScalarToken"]` on arm, the six pinned still cells at
290/839/63/171/580/693 B on both, `regression_spotcheck` 104/104 on both, and
`identity_full_8bit` 1100/1100 on x86. On aarch64 the merged identity run was
KILLED by a harness timeout at 1,020 of its 1,100 cells (0 divergences in
those), so the `dims` tier — the one it was in — was re-run whole and read
**540 / 540**; with the completed `synthetic` tier's 560 that is 1,100 cells of
merged-tree aarch64 coverage, 0 non-identical. Reported as two runs rather than
one because that is what happened. The Ir and wall-clock numbers are NOT
re-taken against `7ec5b557` — they are stated against `84621b20`, which is what
the `base:` line above says.

`main@origin` then advanced AGAIN, to `ab5be1ec` (`percall_layout`, which
touches `overlay.rs`, `predict.rs`, `partition.rs`, `pd0.rs` and `pipeline.rs`
— encoder source, not just benchmarks). Rebased onto it and re-run on BOTH
ISAs: nextest 2546/2546 (arm) / 2556/2556 (x86), the tier witness unchanged on
both, `regression_spotcheck` 104/104 on both, six still cells
290/839/63/171/580/693 B on both. **`identity_full_8bit` was NOT re-run against
`ab5be1ec`** — it is 1,100 cells and the tip is moving faster than that gate
takes; its 1100/1100 stands against the `7ec5b557` tree, and the fast gates
above are what covers `ab5be1ec`. Saying so is the point: the full sweep's
result belongs to the tree it ran on, not to whatever the tip is by the time
the commit lands.

`decode_gate_grid.sh` REFUSED to run rather than skipping: `dav1d not found` on
r7900x, exit 1. That is the tool behaving correctly and it is recorded as a
COVERAGE GAP, not as a pass — the decode evidence for this commit is
`decode_conformance.sh`'s 1260 / 0.

**Memory**, aarch64 (M4 Pro), `tools/mem_gate.sh` — `/usr/bin/time -l` peak RSS,
median of 3 reps, gradient qp 32 preset 6, base binary vs candidate binary built
from the same tree:

  cell                     base KiB   cand KiB   delta      port/C
  2048x2048 still (1 fr)     123,536    123,328  -208 KiB   1.03 -> 1.03  (ident=Y)
  2048x2048 inter (2 fr)     170,448    170,448     0 KiB   0.89 -> 0.89  (ident=N)

No cost. The kernel's stack frame grows from the scalar ring's
`[[u16; 64]; 8]` (1,088 B with its output row) to `[[i32; 64]; 8]` plus the two
i32 scratches (2,688 B) — 1.6 KiB more on ONE leaf frame, which is below this
instrument's resolution and is what the -208 KiB / 0 KiB readings show.
**The aarch64 2048-inter port/C ratio is 0.89, unchanged and far under the
~1.20x ceiling this campaign works to.** The inter cell is `ident=N` (it is
outside the byte-identical frontier), so its two RSS figures are absolute
numbers, not a like-for-like ratio — as the gate's own header requires.

=================== 9. CI ===================

**What CI compiles.** The `avx512` feature is default-on, so every x86-64 job
COMPILES the `_v4` arm — `gates` (ubuntu-latest x86-64) and `pure-rust`
(macos-15-intel) both build it, which makes it a compile-time regression gate
whether or not it executes. On `windows-11-arm` and Apple-silicon hosts the arm
is `#[cfg(target_arch = "x86_64")]`'d out and `_neon` is built instead. On
`i686-unknown-linux-gnu` (via `cross`) `target_arch` is `"x86"`, so `_v4`,
`_v3`, `_neon` and `_wasm128` are ALL cfg'd out and the dispatch reaches
`wiener_convolve_simd_scalar` — correct, and the only tier there.

**What CI runs, and what it does not.** `cargo nextest run --workspace` runs on
`ubuntu-latest`, so the new dsp tests — including the tier witness — execute
there. **Whether the `_v4` arm is among the tiers they exercise depends on that
runner's CPU, and this record does not guess it**: GitHub does not pin the Azure
SKU behind `ubuntu-latest`, and the SKUs it has used range from AVX2-only to
Xeon Platinum parts that do expose AVX-512. The test is written so that either
outcome is correct AND legible: it always asserts `permutations_run >= 2` and
`>= 2` DISTINCT resolved tiers, it prints the tier names it saw, and only the
strong `contains("X64V4Token")` assertion is guarded on
`X64V4Token::summon().is_some()`. So a runner WITHOUT AVX-512 passes on `_v3` +
`_scalar`; a runner WITH it is REQUIRED to reach `_v4` and fails if it does not.
**Read the test's printed tier list in the CI log if you need to know which
happened on a given run** — do not infer it from the runner label.

`pure-rust` (macos-15-intel, windows-11-arm) and `pure-rust-i686` run
`cargo test -p zenav1-svt`, the FACADE, not the dsp crate — so the new unit
tests do not run there. What does run there is `tier_invariance`, an end-to-end
encode asserted byte-identical across every archmage tier the host offers, which
covers this kernel through the encoder rather than directly.

**The `_v4` arm's byte-identity is established on r7900x**, not on CI: the tier
witness resolves `["X64V4Token", "X64V3Token", "ScalarToken"]` there, and the
full gate suite in §8 ran on that host with the arm live.

**One thing NOT measured:** i686's `_scalar` arm is magetypes' array emulation
rather than the old hand-written scalar rows. It is byte-identical (the same
tier sweep proves it) but its SPEED on i686 was not measured, and 32-bit x86 is
not one of the campaign's timing hosts. Do not assume it is faster or slower
than what it replaced.
